                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_106">Cambridge, Cambridgeshire.
                        Peterhouse (Collegium Sancti Petri)</orgName><p>Written for William
                        Dyngley: on fol. 223v in his hand are notes on the expense of producing the
                        book.</p><p>One of the books above which is written in the catalogue of
                        1418, ‘Subscripti libri empti fuerunt ad usum et proficium sociorum collegii
                        cum pecuniis eiusdem collegii’ (CBMLC 10, 474). Beside this entry itself is
                        added, ‘Hos sermones emit Magister Ricardus Bomsted; eos tamen rehabebit
                        collegium post decessum suum prout promisit fideliter’ (CBMLC 10,
                        474).</p><p>Thomson (Peterhouse, 117), writes that ‘The receipt of 20 s.
                        from Bumpstede for ‘xx quaternis sermonum beati Augustini’ is recorded in
                        the College accounts for 1441–2’. However CBMLC 10, 674, records that ‘The
                        purchase price was 20s. but he gave the college a decorated case instead and
                        promised that the book would be returned after his death’. The source for
                        this is not given.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Richard Bumpstede (fl. 1422–1448), fellow of
                     Peterhouse</provenance>
               </history>
            </msDesc>
